  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 257 PM EDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\n===== Wednesday - Thursday =====\n\nTemperatures will continue to warm on Wednesday and Thursday as sfc \nhigh pressure slides east and upper ridging amplifies over the \nregion. The departing sfc high will support a deeper warm air \nadvection flow. Temperatures will climb well into the 80s both days, \nwith another chance to hit 90 by Thursday. We'll remain dry through \nmost of this time, but a shortwave riding across the Central US on \nThursday will push a cold front into the area by Friday. Ahead of \nthis front, we'll see increasing clouds and rain chances arrive by \nThursday night across southern IN. \n\n===== Rainy Period Friday through the Weekend =====\n\nThat cold front will pass through the area on Friday, bringing \nincreasing moisture, clouds, and our next area-wide rain chances. \nTemperatures on Friday will range around 10 degrees north to south, \nwith the cooler temps staying in the upper 70s and lower 80s north \nof the WK/BG Pkwys, but mid to upper 80s to the south where there \nwill be more time to warm up before the front arrives. \n\nSoaking rain, with some embedded thunder, will remain possible \nthrough the weekend as the front lingers across the TN Valley. \nSevere threat remains low given the limited heating Saturday and \nSunday with temps in the 70s. With PWATS surging above 2 inches \nduring this time, moderate to heavy rainfall is possible. QPF \nbetween Friday-Monday could exceed 2-4 inches, but luckily this \nrainfall will be spread out across multiple days. We are in a \nmarginal risk for excessive rainfall by Friday, but expect that to \ncontinue through the weekend too.",
